// A trait cannot declare an associated type in source — a type parameter says the same thing with
// one concept instead of two, and is strictly the more capable of the pair here: a type may
// implement a trait once (a second `impl` is E0027), so there is no ambiguity for the associated
// form to resolve, and the projection that carries it in other languages — `T::Item` through a
// bound — is not expressible.
//
// The mechanism itself is not gone. A NATIVE trait derives its associated types from the
// implementing type's element, which is the one thing a type parameter cannot express because
// there is nothing for the author to write: `impl vec.Kernels for V3 {}` binds `Self::Wide` and
// `Self::Float` from the packed element (see `bundles/kernel_override_body.noe`). That path
// synthesizes its declaration rather than parsing one, so it is untouched by this rule.
// expect: error E0053 at 15:10
// expect: exit 1
trait Container {
    type Item
    fn first(): Item
}

echo 1
